1
00:00:00,974 --> 00:00:06,020
Hi, I'm Sachil Ventlevi, founder of Bloggick Me and WebRTC courses.

2
00:00:06,446 --> 00:00:10,692
I'm here to break down real-time communications so you don't have to.

3
00:00:11,854 --> 00:00:15,460
Time for a short explanation of WebRTC for total beginners.

4
00:00:16,334 --> 00:00:22,852
Now, if you ask me what WebRTC is exactly, this is a definition that I almost always use.

5
00:00:23,534 --> 00:00:28,804
WebRTC offers real-time communications natively from a web browser.

6
00:00:29,486 --> 00:00:34,084
First thing you need to know is that WebRTC is available on all browsers out there.

7
00:00:34,414 --> 00:00:43,460
If you want to use voice or video communications in real time, you can do that from any browser today because we have WebRTC implemented there.

8
00:00:44,142 --> 00:00:50,000
It doesn't mean that WebRTC must run inside the browser, just that this is available for you.

9
00:00:50,160 --> 00:01:00,228
You can also use WebRTC to run it in an application, on a mobile device, on the desktop, a laptop, or even on you know embedded devices.

10
00:01:01,646 --> 00:01:07,140
The second thing is that WebRTC is a media engine with JavaScript APIs.

11
00:01:07,694 --> 00:01:08,800
Why is that important?

12
00:01:09,646 --> 00:01:11,440
WebRTC is a media engine.

13
00:01:11,534 --> 00:01:16,320
We've had media engines before WebRTC for other voiceover IP protocols.

14
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:19,040
We'll in a second see what that means.

15
00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:23,280
But now we have a media engine that has JavaScript APIs for it.

16
00:01:23,566 --> 00:01:33,188
The APIs are implemented and available in web browsers, so any developer can actually go and use these same APIs to build their own applications.

17
00:01:33,806 --> 00:01:44,324
So WebRTC creates an ecosystem around it by having a single set of APIs of how it is used and implemented, and by making it available in all browsers.

18
00:01:46,126 --> 00:01:49,572
What I've said so far is voice over IP, web browsers.

19
00:01:50,062 --> 00:01:51,556
That brings us to another point.

20
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:58,820
If we look at WebRTC, it's right between traditional voiceover IP and our internet, the web.

21
00:01:59,374 --> 00:02:02,720
Now, voice over IP and the web are two different things.

22
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:04,240
They're two different animals.

23
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:08,548
I come from the voice over IP domain, let's call it.

24
00:02:08,974 --> 00:02:20,740
This is where you learn things about standards and how you may make calls and what's ringing and transfer and blind transfer and other words that relate to the world of telephony.

25
00:02:21,486 --> 00:02:33,924
In the web, we look at things in different ways, with JavaScript and scaling horizontally, not vertically, and doing things in ways that are very different than voice over IP.

26
00:02:34,638 --> 00:02:47,012
Since WebRTC is right in the middle, we need to understand some of the things of VoIP and some of the things of the web in order to make sure that we are building things in a way that makes sense to both of these worlds at the same time.

27
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:51,748
Let's go and see how calls are actually made with WebRTC.

28
00:02:53,038 --> 00:02:54,948
So we have a user.

29
00:02:55,598 --> 00:02:56,612
Let's call him A.

30
00:02:57,134 --> 00:03:01,700
That user runs on a web browser, on his laptop, like I am now.

31
00:03:02,542 --> 00:03:05,796
He wants to call or to communicate with someone else with B.

32
00:03:06,734 --> 00:03:09,988
B is actually running on a smartphone, on a device.

33
00:03:10,510 --> 00:03:12,880
She might be doing it in a browser or an application.

34
00:03:13,070 --> 00:03:15,172
We don't really care for this example.

35
00:03:15,918 --> 00:03:21,860
Now, how does A and B know that they exist or that how can they even find each other?

36
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:26,640
The answer to that is through something that is called a signaling server.

37
00:03:27,054 --> 00:03:30,916
Both users are connected to a signaling server.

38
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:39,076
They are both, they both made the decision that they want to communicate with each other and that they are going to do it based on a specific network.

39
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:49,188
It might be WhatsApp or Messenger or FaceTime or a Google Meet call or a third party of some kind or whatever other service.

40
00:03:49,742 --> 00:03:58,884
There is a decision that was made that is capable of the discovery part of making sure that when A wants to connect to B, he can actually do that.

41
00:03:59,630 --> 00:04:02,308
So, what happens now when A actually wants to connect to B?

42
00:04:03,214 --> 00:04:07,108
He sends a message, an offer to the signaling server.

43
00:04:07,792 --> 00:04:11,140
That offer message is not part of WebRTC.

44
00:04:11,232 --> 00:04:18,116
It is done out of the scope of WebRTC, which again complicates things a bit but brings a lot of flexibility.

45
00:04:18,958 --> 00:04:27,684
That offer message that is being sent has a piece of data in it called SDP, session description protocol, which is part of WebRTC.

46
00:04:28,238 --> 00:04:34,432
Inside the SDP, A says things like, Well, you know, B, I want to do a video call with you.

47
00:04:34,766 --> 00:04:42,832
These are the ways that I can do audio, these are the ways that I can do video, these are the resolutions that I'm supporting, these are the codecs that I use.

48
00:04:42,992 --> 00:04:45,792
Codecs are used for compressing audio and video.

49
00:04:45,952 --> 00:04:52,388
The different codecs would bring different compression ratios and different qualities to the actual results.

50
00:04:52,752 --> 00:04:56,996
Okay, so we've got an offer that is being sent with the SDP.

51
00:04:57,454 --> 00:04:59,472
The signaling server received this offer.

52
00:04:59,758 --> 00:05:03,712
He says, Well, this is a message that A wants to communicate with B.

53
00:05:04,046 --> 00:05:07,748
So the signaling server is going to send that offer to B.

54
00:05:08,910 --> 00:05:10,820
Now B is going to look at that offer.

55
00:05:11,086 --> 00:05:17,380
In this point in time, the phone might be ringing because that's what the application decided to do with that incoming offer.

56
00:05:17,872 --> 00:05:26,404
Once B decides to accept this call or this meeting, he is going to send back or she is going to send back an answer.

57
00:05:27,150 --> 00:05:34,112
The answer is again an SDP message with an answer component to it that says, Well, you know what?

58
00:05:34,192 --> 00:05:38,672
These are the things that I support, and these are the things that I selected from what you are supporting.

59
00:05:38,894 --> 00:05:40,132
Let's start a call.

60
00:05:40,592 --> 00:05:45,764
And again, that message itself, besides the SDP part, is out of scope of WebRTC.

61
00:05:45,952 --> 00:05:49,156
How it is being sent, you decide in your application.

62
00:05:50,352 --> 00:05:57,472
The signaling server receives the answer, checks whatever he wants about that, and then he sends that to A.

63
00:05:58,894 --> 00:06:07,204
At this point, both A and B have two SDP components or two SDP blobs of the offer and the answer.

64
00:06:07,726 --> 00:06:12,164
They now use these SDP blobs in order to make magic happen.

65
00:06:12,494 --> 00:06:22,308
And that magic is sending media directly from A to B and from B to A, having an actual voice or video call between these two participants.

66
00:06:22,832 --> 00:06:24,672
And this is done peer-to-peer.

67
00:06:25,134 --> 00:06:33,872
The actual media is sent directly from one user to another, not going through the signaling server at all or through any other server for that matter.

68
00:06:34,222 --> 00:06:40,932
This is why in many cases people refer to WebRTC as a peer-to-peer technology because it doesn't require servers.

69
00:06:41,486 --> 00:06:42,212
But it does.

70
00:06:42,574 --> 00:06:47,140
We've already seen one server that WebRTC requires, which is the signaling server.

71
00:06:48,238 --> 00:06:51,940
Now, WebRTC for me is like a kind of an iceberg.

72
00:06:52,558 --> 00:06:59,632
There are things at the top that we see, like this nice drawing and the fact that messages go back and forth and now we have a call, yay!

73
00:06:59,950 --> 00:07:03,524
And then there is a lot that happens below the water level.

74
00:07:03,854 --> 00:07:06,724
These are things that we usually miss with WebRTC.

75
00:07:07,086 --> 00:07:10,724
We're going to go deep a bit to see these things now.

76
00:07:11,392 --> 00:07:15,952
Because WebRTC is peer to peer, but it requires a signaling server.

77
00:07:16,206 --> 00:07:25,284
WebRTC is peer to peer, but there are other servers with it we might need, and the media might not go peer to peer, it might actually go through a server.

78
00:07:26,350 --> 00:07:29,732
It brings me to the point of what servers do we actually have in WebRTC?

79
00:07:30,382 --> 00:07:31,876
We've seen the signaling server.

80
00:07:32,910 --> 00:07:39,172
In many cases, in actual real applications, we are going to also have an application server.

81
00:07:39,854 --> 00:07:43,232
We're going to decouple the signaling from the actual application logic.

82
00:07:43,392 --> 00:07:56,192
We'll have the application logic connected to the database, for example, and to things that we want to do, authentication and other mechanisms and things that we need in our application, like the web page itself.

83
00:07:56,558 --> 00:08:07,364
And the signaling server is going to take care of the real-time stuff of keeping the connections open between the users in the network, having the ability to understand at what state they are.

84
00:08:07,790 --> 00:08:13,712
Are they now in a call, waiting for a call, can be called, busy, whatever it is that we want to convey.

85
00:08:13,872 --> 00:08:17,872
This is part of the things that the signaling server is going to be in charge of.

86
00:08:18,032 --> 00:08:21,792
We might co-locate the signaling with the application server and we might not.

87
00:08:21,952 --> 00:08:22,628
It's up to us.

88
00:08:24,014 --> 00:08:29,764
Now we said that WebRTC is peer-to-peer and media flows directly between A and B.

89
00:08:30,352 --> 00:08:41,892
But sometimes firewalls and NATS, network address translators, which are kind of switches on the network, are not going to allow us to send media directly from A to B.

90
00:08:42,510 --> 00:08:50,980
In such a case, we have Stan and TurnServer's ICE protocols that enables us to make that communication happen.

91
00:08:51,502 --> 00:09:15,472
The STAN server, for example, will allow A and B to understand and find their public IP addresses and use that to announce where they can be found, so that once we are trying to do the ICE negotiation to connect this call in the media between A and B, we can check using that public IP address instead of the private one that we would have used.

92
00:09:15,472 --> 00:09:15,876
otherwise.

93
00:09:16,622 --> 00:09:19,076
With turn, we're going to do something else.

94
00:09:19,694 --> 00:09:24,836
We're going to relay the media through a turn server instead of going direct.

95
00:09:25,742 --> 00:09:29,956
We do that when there is no other way of connecting A to B directly.

96
00:09:30,926 --> 00:09:42,244
So WebRTC is peer-to-peer, but well, media might not actually flow directly, even if you wanted to, because our firewalls are nuts, and now we need to relay that through turn servers.

97
00:09:42,830 --> 00:09:45,060
Okay, so it's not always peer-to-peer.

98
00:09:46,094 --> 00:09:54,832
The other thing is that you might not want to have it run peer-to-peer directly because you might want to introduce media servers into the mix.

99
00:09:55,182 --> 00:10:03,044
With media servers, I can do things like group calling, have a call between 10 participants, something that I cannot really do peer-to-peer.

100
00:10:03,406 --> 00:10:06,564
We don't have enough bandwidth and CPU power in devices for that.

101
00:10:07,022 --> 00:10:09,636
Then we put media servers in other times.

102
00:10:10,126 --> 00:10:13,508
I want to do things like record the meeting and store it in the cloud.

103
00:10:13,806 --> 00:10:15,524
I would do that with a media server.

104
00:10:16,526 --> 00:10:23,812
I might want to connect a bot, an LLM, generative AI, you know, do these kinds of things.

105
00:10:24,270 --> 00:10:28,196
Then again, I need a media server to connect to a cloud-based AI.

106
00:10:28,846 --> 00:10:39,556
I might want to connect to a telephony service, having an agent sit in his home with a laptop using WebRTC, and have people call him on the phone.

107
00:10:40,366 --> 00:10:44,420
In order for them to call on the phone, they're not going to use WebRTC, they're going to use telephony.

108
00:10:44,878 --> 00:10:51,140
And then we have a media server called the Gateway that is going to connect from telephony into WebRTC.

109
00:10:51,632 --> 00:10:56,516
So there are many reasons why WebRTC is not really peer-to-peer in a lot of different use cases.

110
00:10:56,878 --> 00:11:08,772
And to be frank, most services that are production ready, running at scale, that are successful, require media servers for some of the things that they do, if not all of the things that they do.

111
00:11:09,312 --> 00:11:10,372
Bear that in mind.

112
00:11:11,152 --> 00:11:13,312
So you've seen a bit how things work here.

113
00:11:13,472 --> 00:11:16,100
We've seen that there is an offer answer message.

114
00:11:16,494 --> 00:11:19,152
We've seen that the media runs directly or indirectly.

115
00:11:19,438 --> 00:11:23,844
We've seen that there are different types of servers in the network when we use WebRTC.

116
00:11:24,192 --> 00:11:30,084
We haven't discussed the ICE negotiation process, how we connect, the state machines in WebRTC.

117
00:11:30,192 --> 00:11:32,132
There's a lot more to learn in WebRTC.

118
00:11:32,592 --> 00:11:35,396
I want to touch a few more points before we finish today.

119
00:11:36,366 --> 00:11:43,152
First one, this is WebRTC's protocol stack taken from the great book, High Performance Browser Networking.

120
00:11:43,406 --> 00:11:46,404
This is a rather old book, but is still good today.

121
00:11:47,214 --> 00:11:50,272
You can see a lot of different protocols that exist in WebRTC.

122
00:11:50,352 --> 00:11:55,172
WebRTC is a standard that has multiple protocols wrapped into it.

123
00:11:55,694 --> 00:11:58,112
These protocols are not specific to WebRTC.

124
00:11:58,192 --> 00:12:01,188
WebRTC uses them in a very specific way.

125
00:12:02,222 --> 00:12:07,520
Now, the right hand side of this diagram holds WebRTC implementation itself.

126
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:09,920
We've got there i stand and turn.

127
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,200
We've seen these already.

128
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:15,652
Here there are the client side implementations of these servers.

129
00:12:16,590 --> 00:12:22,852
And IC is the algorithm that does this negotiation and makes sure that we actually can connect the call no matter the network.

130
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:28,324
Then we've got the layer for DLS, which adds security on top of UDP.

131
00:12:28,750 --> 00:12:36,720
And we have SRTP, secure RTP, real-time protocol, which is used to actually send the audio and video in real time.

132
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:43,780
This is a known voice over IP protocol that is used almost across all voice over IP protocols and implementations.

133
00:12:44,462 --> 00:12:48,132
And then there is SATP, which is used for the data channel.

134
00:12:48,494 --> 00:12:55,840
The data channel enables us to send whatever we want directly between the two peers, not specifically audio and video.

135
00:12:56,014 --> 00:13:04,132
So we can send things like keyboard presses, mouse moves, telemetry, images, or other data that we want to send.

136
00:13:04,558 --> 00:13:10,148
And yes, we can also send audio and video over SATP and the data channel if we really want to.

137
00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:13,360
The left-hand side is interesting.

138
00:13:14,062 --> 00:13:18,340
The author of the book decided to show that as part of WebRTC, although it isn't.

139
00:13:18,862 --> 00:13:24,644
Why it's not, because this is what you have in the browser to enable signaling for WebRTC.

140
00:13:25,166 --> 00:13:36,164
So if I want to send that offer and answer messages or any other additional messages like on ice candidates and you know disconnect calls and things like that, I need a signaling channel.

141
00:13:36,462 --> 00:13:44,164
And since we said that that's out of scope of WebRTC, the way we're usually going to do that are by ways that are available in the browser.

142
00:13:44,974 --> 00:13:50,052
And these ways for signaling are XHR, SSC, and WebSockets.

143
00:13:50,638 --> 00:13:53,316
XHR and SSC runs on top of HTTP.

144
00:13:53,774 --> 00:13:58,560
In the case of WebRTC, that's going to be HTTPS, which is why I have TLS in there.

145
00:13:58,766 --> 00:14:03,556
And that's going to happen on top of TCP or on top of UDP with HTTP3 today.

146
00:14:04,590 --> 00:14:10,240
So we've got all of the media stack running on top of UDP most of the time, and this is what we want.

147
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:13,360
That's where real time thrives and works really, really well.

148
00:14:13,614 --> 00:14:18,916
And the signaling usually runs on TCP because we need reliability for these messages.

149
00:14:20,558 --> 00:14:24,548
When it comes to security, WebRTC is secure by design.

150
00:14:25,262 --> 00:14:34,692
It doesn't mean that you don't need to deal with security or think about that, but it means that WebRTC in a way forces you to deal with security and to work with security.

151
00:14:34,990 --> 00:14:36,004
How it does that?

152
00:14:36,846 --> 00:14:37,360
Simple.

153
00:14:38,222 --> 00:14:40,964
The actual SRTP protocol is secure RTP.

154
00:14:41,326 --> 00:14:45,988
It comes in a way that you cannot send media if it's not encrypted on the network.

155
00:14:46,478 --> 00:14:47,108
That's one.

156
00:14:47,950 --> 00:14:51,396
SATP runs on top of the TLS, which means that it's secure as well.

157
00:14:52,334 --> 00:14:56,100
On the other side of the stack where you don't have over TC, there's TLS.

158
00:14:56,942 --> 00:15:00,004
It says here that it is optional, but not exactly.

159
00:15:01,102 --> 00:15:10,080
It is optional, but if you actually use it in that way and say, well, I don't want TLS, the browsers are going to make your life really, really hard in the user experience.

160
00:15:10,254 --> 00:15:20,004
So we're going to end up running secure sockets or secure web sockets or HTTP, the secure HTTP variant of HTTP.

161
00:15:20,558 --> 00:15:23,492
And then TLS is kind of mandatory here.

162
00:15:23,982 --> 00:15:30,720
So RTC is secure by design, and then your application, you need to take care of the security of the application.

163
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:35,492
But at least the media engine that you use comes with security in mind.

164
00:15:38,318 --> 00:15:44,196
The last thing I want to touch today is the fact that you need to understand that you are not in control when it comes to WebRTC.

165
00:15:44,832 --> 00:15:47,108
There are four different actors in WebRTC.

166
00:15:47,632 --> 00:15:49,988
The first one is your application.

167
00:15:50,414 --> 00:15:57,188
You're building the application, you're making the decision there, it's yours, you control the application totally and fully.

168
00:15:58,254 --> 00:15:59,588
Then there are the browsers.

169
00:16:00,334 --> 00:16:02,532
The browsers, you don't control them.

170
00:16:02,912 --> 00:16:09,348
These come from Google, from Apple, from Microsoft, from Mozilla, and from other companies.

171
00:16:10,478 --> 00:16:13,152
All of these browsers are out of your control.

172
00:16:14,350 --> 00:16:20,100
Google can update Chrome without asking you, and it happens all the time, on a monthly basis.

173
00:16:20,846 --> 00:16:21,072
Okay?

174
00:16:21,392 --> 00:16:29,552
Things change, the behavior of WebRTC implementations change, and you need to keep track of that and be up to speed with these things.

175
00:16:29,902 --> 00:16:37,476
This is going to affect how you're going to do the QA, how you're going to deliver bug fixes, and how you're going to do all of the upgrades of your system.

176
00:16:39,758 --> 00:16:40,964
Then there's the networks.

177
00:16:42,190 --> 00:16:53,152
Users connect from whatever network they're on: Wi-Fi, Ethernet, cellular, inside basements, elevators, their homes, hotels, hospitals, whatnot.

178
00:16:53,486 --> 00:16:56,164
You don't know what networks your users are using.

179
00:16:56,526 --> 00:16:59,684
In many use cases, you don't control or own the networks.

180
00:17:00,112 --> 00:17:02,500
In some, very few, you do.

181
00:17:03,160 --> 00:17:06,512
WebRTC has mechanisms to deal with different types of networks.

182
00:17:06,830 --> 00:17:09,380
Your application needs to be aware of that as well.

183
00:17:10,606 --> 00:17:14,148
Last but not least, are the users' devices and peripherals?

184
00:17:14,702 --> 00:17:15,792
What device are they using?

185
00:17:15,952 --> 00:17:17,072
Is it new or old?

186
00:17:17,326 --> 00:17:20,580
Is it a laptop or a smartphone or a tablet or an embedded device?

187
00:17:20,912 --> 00:17:22,912
What kind of camera and microphone do they have?

188
00:17:23,054 --> 00:17:23,792
What are the speakers?

189
00:17:23,952 --> 00:17:25,312
Is there a screen resolution?

190
00:17:25,518 --> 00:17:26,372
The CPU.

191
00:17:26,702 --> 00:17:29,072
These things are going to affect the user experience.

192
00:17:29,232 --> 00:17:30,512
You need to be aware of that.

193
00:17:30,672 --> 00:17:37,632
And again, in most cases, in most scenarios, you are not going to control these devices.

194
00:17:37,712 --> 00:17:42,148
You just need to be able to work with a variety of things that are going to come your way.

195
00:17:45,006 --> 00:17:46,148
We've seen WebRTC.

196
00:17:46,830 --> 00:17:52,516
We now understand how it works a bit and a bit about the ecosystem around it and the main concepts of that.

197
00:17:52,992 --> 00:17:57,152
If you want to learn more, you can follow me at bloggeek.me.

198
00:17:57,582 --> 00:18:00,752
You can also check my courses at WebRTCcourse.com.

199
00:18:00,832 --> 00:18:11,812
There are free courses there around WebRTC basics and the WebRTC Code Lab that will take you hand in hand to build your first WebRTC application with an explanation of how to use the APIs.

200
00:18:12,238 --> 00:18:13,028
So see you there.

